#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Refuse a firmware change that documents nothing.
#
# Not bureaucracy: docs/ROADMAP.md has gone stale twice, once within a day of
# being written. A roadmap nobody trusts is worse than none, because it gets
# read anyway.
#
# This is deliberately a block with an escape rather than a warning. A warning
# gets ignored; a block forces a decision. When the change genuinely needs no
# documentation - a typo, a rename, a formatting pass - say so explicitly:
#
#     SKIP_DOC_CHECK=1 git commit ...
#
# Installed via `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`, so it travels with the
# repository instead of living in an unversioned .git/hooks.
#
# NOTE: this file must stay tracked in git. It was silently lost once by a
# `git reset --hard` during its own testing, and every commit for two days
# passed unchecked because the documentation describing it was committed while
# the hook itself was not.

set -o pipefail

[ -n "$SKIP_DOC_CHECK" ] && exit 0

staged=$(git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR)
[ -z "$staged" ] && exit 0

code=$(printf '%s\n' "$staged" | grep -E '^(src/|include/|lib/|tools/|platformio\.ini)' || true)
docs=$(printf '%s\n' "$staged" | grep -E '^(docs/|CLAUDE\.md|README\.md|CONTRIBUTING\.md)' || true)

[ -z "$code" ] && exit 0
[ -n "$docs" ] && exit 0

cat >&2 <<'MSG'

  Firmware changed, no documentation did.

  CONTRIBUTING.md decides where a fact belongs:

    a fact about the board ............ docs/HARDWARE.md
    a rule code must obey ............. docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
    a failure you diagnosed ........... docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md  (by symptom)
    a patch to someone else's code .... docs/PORTING_NOTES.md
    an alternative you rejected ....... docs/decisions/
    work finished, or newly found ..... docs/ROADMAP.md

  If this closes a roadmap item, move it to that file's "Done" section with
  today's date - do not just delete the line. If it opens one, add it.

  Nothing to document? Then say so:

    SKIP_DOC_CHECK=1 git commit ...

MSG
exit 1
